== 7.1.1 Game-Specific Facts

Standard association football is normally played by two teams of up to eleven players on the field, including a goalkeeper. A goal is scored when the whole ball crosses the goal line between the posts and under the crossbar, provided no infringement has occurred. A throw-in restarts play after the whole ball crosses a touchline; a corner kick or goal kick may follow the ball crossing the goal line outside a goal, depending on who last touched it. The offside law requires attention to position and involvement in active play; simply standing in an offside position is not itself an offence.

In standard basketball, five players from each team play on court. A player normally advances by passing or dribbling, subject to travelling and dribbling rules. A field goal's value depends on the shot and the applicable rules; a free throw normally counts one point. In indoor volleyball, six players from each team are on court. Teams aim to ground the ball in the opponents' court; the rules normally allow up to three team hits to return it, with a block treated separately in the standard code. Adapted or school formats can differ.

Under the conventional 21-point badminton format used in 2026, a game is played to 21 points: at 20–20 a side needs a two-point lead, with the game capped at 30 points. A match is normally best of three games. A 15-point best-of-three international format has been approved for implementation from 4 January 2027: at 14–14 a two-point lead is needed, but 21 wins at 20–20. Events may use approved alternatives. Check the rules and date specified in a question or competition; school formats can also differ.

== 7.2 Basic Skills and Teamwork

A *pass* transfers a ball to another player; *receiving* involves controlling an incoming ball. *Dribbling* has a game-specific meaning: the action and restrictions in basketball are not identical to those in football. A *serve* starts play or a rally in relevant games, subject to that game's rules.

Skill development involves practice, feedback and decision-making. A player must also observe the position of teammates, opponents and space. Cooperation and communication can support play, but a useful pass must still comply with the rules of the particular game.

