== 7.3 Officials and Competitions

Officials help apply the rules, manage play and record outcomes according to their assigned responsibilities. A referee, umpire, scorer or timekeeper may have different duties depending on the sport and competition. Organisers also plan venues, schedules, equipment, accessibility and safety.

Competitions can be organised in formats such as a *league*, where participants play a scheduled series, or a *knockout*, where a participant is eliminated after a loss under the format's rules. Exact procedures, tie-breaks and qualification conditions must be checked for the particular competition. National and international bodies may organise or govern different competitions; their names and rules should be verified using current official sources.

#concept-box("League and knockout")[
  A league usually uses a series of scheduled fixtures. A knockout format generally eliminates a participant after a loss. Actual competitions can combine formats or use additional rules.
]

== 7.4 Fair Play and Safety

Fair play includes respecting rules, officials and other participants, avoiding deliberate harm and accepting decisions through the appropriate process. Winning does not justify cheating, harassment or unsafe action. A coach or teacher should match activities to the learners, inspect equipment and encourage reporting of injuries or concerning symptoms.

Rules also support safety, but following a rulebook does not eliminate every hazard. Check the playing area, equipment and supervision. Adaptations can allow meaningful participation while preserving a fair and safe activity. Explain the agreed boundaries and any modified rules to all participants before play, and review whether those changes support the intended learning goal.

== 7.5 Four Worked Examples
#worked-example(1, "Choose the equipment", tier: "FOUNDATION",
  [Which game commonly uses a shuttlecock? \
A. Badminton \
B. Basketball \
C. Football \
D. Shot put],
  [Badminton uses rackets and a shuttlecock. The answer is *A*.])
#worked-example(2, "Transfer to a teammate", tier: "UTME STANDARD",
  [Sending the ball to a teammate is a \
A. posture diagnosis \
B. pass \
C. fracture \
D. sport federation],
  [A pass transfers the ball to a teammate. The answer is *B*.])
#worked-example(3, "A competition format", tier: "UTME STANDARD",
  [A format in which a participant is generally eliminated after a loss is \
A. a warm-up \
B. a muscle type \
C. knockout \
D. an exchange baton],
  [A knockout format generally eliminates a participant after a loss, subject to the event rules. The answer is *C*.])
#worked-example(4, "Fair play", tier: "JAMB CHALLENGE",
  [A player disagrees with a decision. The most appropriate general response is to \
A. deliberately injure an opponent \
B. change the score secretly \
C. disregard all rules \
D. use the competition's appropriate process and respect other participants],
  [Fair play includes respect and use of the appropriate process for disagreements. The answer is *D*.])

#trap-box("A shared word can hide different rules")[
  Dribbling, serving and fouls are governed by the rules of the particular game. Do not transfer a rule from one sport to another without checking.
]

